  "name": "Prawn Noodle Soup - Penang Hokkien Mee",
  "text": "Prawn Noodle Soup - Penang Hokkien Mee\n\nYield: 4-6\n\nPreparation time: 20 mins\n\nCooking time: 2¼ hours\n\nStatus: Untried\n\nIngredients\n\nFor the stock\n\n3 tbsp dried shrimp,\n\n1 dried red chilli,\n\n50 g fresh root ginger (peeled and sliced),\n\n2 onions (quartered),\n\n4 cloves of garlic (bruised),\n\n2 lemongrass stalks (bruised),\n\n½ tsp black peppercorns,\n\n2-3 tbsp dark soy sauce,\n\n700 g pork and chicken bones (or pork ribs and chicken wings),\n\nHeads and shells of prawns.\n\nFor the soup\n\n1 tbsp sugar,\n\n6 rashers sliced bacon (or cubed pork belly),\n\n150 g fresh egg noodles,\n\n20 large prawns (peeled, reserving shells for stock),\n\n90 g beansprouts,\n\n2 spring onions (trimmed and finely sliced),\n\nSalt and pepper.\n\nPreparation\n\n1. Put all the stock ingredients into a deep pan along with the prawn shells.\n\n2. Pour in 2 l of water and bring to the boil.\n\n3. Reduce the heat and simmer gently, uncovered, for about 2 hours, until the stock has reduced by half.\n\n4. Strain the stock into a fresh pan and put on a low heat to keep warm.\n\n5. Season the stock with salt and pepper.\n\n6. In a small pan heat the sugar with 15 ml of water until it turns rich and brown.\n\n7. Add the sugar to the stock and mix well.\n\n8. In a heavy pan deep fry the bacon or pork belly and set aside.\n\n9. Using a perforated ladle of sieve, plunge the noodles into the hot stock for 1 minute to heat through.\n\n10. Remove the noodles and divide into the bowls.\n\n11. Add the prawns to the stock and cook for 1 minute, then remove with a slotted spoon and divide between the bowls.\n\n12. Add the beansprouts to the bowls.\n\n13. Ladle the stock into the bowls.\n\n14 Garnish with bacon and spring onions.",
  "recipeYield": "4-6",
  "prepTime": "PT20M",
